  "question": {
    "text": "The distance of the line $\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y-6}{3}=\\frac{z-3}{4}$ from the point $(1,4,0)$ along the line $\\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{y-2}{2}=\\frac{z+3}{3}$ is :",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "L4QDZ",
      "text": "$\\sqrt{17}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "K9p9b",
      "text": "$\\sqrt{15}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "inlTx",
      "text": "$\\sqrt{14}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "8B5od",
      "text": "$\\sqrt{13}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Line passing through $(1,4,0)$ and parallel to $\\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{y-2}{2}=\\frac{z+3}{3}$ is $L: \\frac{x-1}{1}=\\frac{y-4}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}$\r\nAny point on $L:(\\lambda+1,2 \\lambda+4,3 \\lambda)$\r\nAny point on $\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y-6}{3}=\\frac{z-3}{4}$ is $(2 \\mu+2,3 \\mu+6,4 \\mu+3)$\r\n$\\left.\\begin{array}{l}\r\n\\lambda+1=2 \\mu+2 \\\\\r\n2 \\lambda+4=3 \\mu+6 \\\\\r\n3 \\lambda=4 \\mu+3\r\n\\end{array}\\right\\} \\lambda=1 \\mu=0$\r\nPoint: $(2,6,3)$\r\n$\\begin{aligned}\r\n& \\text { Distance }=\\sqrt{(2-1)^2+(6-4)^2+(3-0)^2} \\\\\r\n& =\\sqrt{1+4+9}=\\sqrt{14}\r\n\\end{aligned}$",
    "image": null
  },
